-- title:
--   Endomorphism dictionary extended to the Hecke element s
-- statement:
--   **Numerical data.** Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, a non-zero natural number $N$ divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$ and squarefree (`hrN`, `hrbarN`, `hN`), and a natural number $n \geq 3$ divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$ and coprime to $N$ (`hn`, `hrn`, `hrbarn`, `hnN`).
--
--   **Coefficients.** $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring, $\pi \in \mathcal O$ is irreducible, $\mathcal O$ is complete for the $\pi$-adic topology, the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements and $(r) = (\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$; $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field of fractions of $\mathcal O$. Further, `Onr` is a characteristic-zero domain and $\mathcal O$-algebra, complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, that ideal is maximal, every element of `Onr` satisfies, modulo that ideal, a monic polynomial with coefficients in $\mathcal O$, and every monic polynomial of positive degree over `Onr` has a root modulo that ideal. Throughout, $k_0$ denotes the residue ring `Onr ⧸ Ideal.span {algebraMap 𝒪 Onr π}`, which is the base over which all schemes below live.
--
--   **The indefinite side.** The hypothesis `hB` asserts that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is indefinite ($0 < a$ or $0 < b$) and that for a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ the algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies over $r$ or over $\bar r$. Here $\Lambda$ is a maximal order (an order maximal for inclusion among orders) containing every rational integer (`hΛℤ`), and `coord` is a map $\Lambda \to \mathbb{Z}_r^{(2)} \times \mathbb{Z}_r^{(2)}$, where $\mathbb{Z}_r^{(2)}$ is the Witt ring of the quadratic extension of $\mathbb F_r$, satisfying `IsOrderCoord`: it is additive, sends $1$ to $(1,0)$, is injective, satisfies the twisted multiplication rule $\mathrm{coord}(mm') = (c_1c_1' + r\,c_2\varphi(c_2'),\; c_1c_2' + c_2\varphi(c_1'))$ for $\mathrm{coord}(m) = (c_1,c_2)$, $\mathrm{coord}(m') = (c_1',c_2')$ and $\varphi$ the Witt-vector Frobenius, has dense image modulo every power of $r$, and satisfies the trace clause: if $m + \bar m$ is the rational integer $n$ then $c_1 + \varphi(c_1) = n$.
--
--   **The definite side.** The hypothesis `hdef` asserts that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ is definite ($a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$) and ramified exactly at $\bar r$. Here $\Lambda_1$ is a maximal order, $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders, of relative index $N$ in one of them) with $R_1 \leq \Lambda_1$; $\iota_0$ is an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$; $v$ is a height-one prime of the ring of integers of $\mathbb Q$ containing $r$; and $\tilde\Gamma$ (written `Γt`) is a subgroup of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ whose elements are exactly those of [`CerednikDrinfeld.CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v`](def/CerednikDrinfeld_CosetGraphAtPrime.html#L37), that is, the units whose image in the localisation at each height-one prime $w \neq v$ lies in the subgroup generated by the local box units of $R_1$ at $w$.
--
--   **The base datum over $k_0$.** $A_0$ is a fake elliptic curve for $(\Lambda,N)$ over $k_0$ (an abelian scheme of relative dimension $2$ with commutative relative group law, a $\Lambda$-action compatible with the group law and the trace condition, and a level-$N$ datum `lev`); $X_0$ is a special formal $r$-divisible module over $k_0$ of height $4$ in the sense `hX₀ : X₀.HasHeight 4`; $\theta_0$ are two-dimensional formal coordinates for the structure morphism of $A_0$; $e, e' : \tilde\Gamma \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ with $e\gamma$ over the base (`he`), and $\deg : \tilde\Gamma \to \mathbb N$. The hypothesis `hA₀` says that $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates for the group law of $A_0$ along $X_0.F$ and that, on nilpotent points, the action of $m \in \Lambda$ is computed by the series $\mathrm{addVia}\,(X_0.\mathrm{act}\,c_1,\; (X_0.\mathrm{act}\,c_2)\circ X_0.\varpi)$ for $\mathrm{coord}(m) = (c_1,c_2)$.
--
--   **The dictionary hypotheses at the base point.** `hE1`: for each $\gamma$, $(e\gamma, e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg \gamma}$ (both are homomorphisms for the group laws, commute with the $\Lambda$-action, and compose to multiplication by $r^{\deg\gamma}$ in both orders), and $e\gamma$ carries level-$N$ points to level-$N$ points. `hE1mul`: $e(\gamma\gamma')$ agrees with $e\gamma' \text{ followed by } e\gamma$ after composing with suitable multiplications by powers of $r$. `hE1sc`: if $\gamma$ is the scalar $c \in \mathbb Z$ then $e\gamma$ agrees with multiplication by $c\,r^i$ after composing with multiplication by $r^i$, for some $i$. `hE1'`: conversely, if $e\gamma$ agrees up to a power of $r$ with multiplication by a positive integer, then $\gamma$ is a rational scalar. `hE2`: for every injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of $\{X_0.\mathrm{actEnd}\,a\} \cup \{X_0.\varpi\mathrm{End}\}$ in $\operatorname{End}(X_0.F)$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is a single $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that every $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ admits $\varepsilon$ in that centraliser and $k_\gamma \in \mathbb Z$ with: for every $k_0$-algebra $B'$, every ideal $J$ with $J^{m+1} = 0$ and every $s$ with entries in $J$, the truncated evaluation of the power series of $\varepsilon$ at $s$ is carried by $\theta_0$ to the image of $\theta_0(s)$ under $e\gamma$; and $E_0\varepsilon = r^{k_\gamma}\,g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$. `hE3`: every full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$ admits a labelling $\mathrm{lab} : \tilde\Gamma \to \Lambda$ with $e\gamma$ acting on $P_0$ as $\mathrm{lab}(\gamma)$ does, multiplicative modulo $n\Lambda$, and matching scalars modulo $n\Lambda$. `hE4`: for every algebraically closed field $k$ that is a $k_0$-algebra, every fake elliptic curve $A$ over $k$ and every $g_A : A.A \to A_0.A$ exhibiting $A$ as the base change of $A_0$ along $k_0 \to k$ in the sense of `IsPullbackVia`, every level-preserving isogeny pair of $r$-power degree on $A$ is, after composition with powers of $r$ and with $g_A$, induced by some $e\gamma$.
--
--   **The order-level dictionary.** $R_2 \leq R_1$ is an order such that every element of $R_1$ multiplied by a suitable power of $r$ lies in $R_2$ (`hR₂r`), and $\hat e : R_2 \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ has all $\hat e(x)$ over the base. The clauses are: `hE5a` (each $\hat e(x)$ is a homomorphism for the group law, commutes with the $\Lambda$-action, and preserves level); `hE5one` ($\hat e(1) = \mathrm{id}$); `hE5mul` ($\hat e(xy) = \hat e(y)$ followed by $\hat e(x)$); `hE5int` (integers act by multiplication); `hE5c` ($\hat e(\bar x)$ followed by $\hat e(x)$ is multiplication by $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$ whenever that reduced norm is the integer $n_x$); `hE5d` (if $x = r^k\gamma$ then $e\gamma$ and $\hat e(x)$ agree after composition with suitable powers of $r$); `hE5e` (the joint form of `hE2`: one $g$ serving simultaneously all $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ and all $x \in R_2$, with $E_0\varepsilon = r^{k_x} g\,\iota_0(x)\,g^{-1}$ in the second case); and `hE5g` ($R_2$ is stable under the quaternion conjugation $\mathrm{star}$).
--
--   **The twin order.** $\Lambda_1^s$ is a maximal order with $R_1 \leq \Lambda_1^s$ and $\Lambda_1 \cap \Lambda_1^s = R_1$; $R_2' \leq \Lambda_1^s$ is an order with $R_2 \leq R_2'$ into which every element of $\Lambda_1^s$ is carried by multiplication by a power of $r$; and $\hat e' : R_2' \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ has all $\hat e'(z)$ over the base. The clauses `hE6a` (homomorphism for the group law and commuting with the $\Lambda$-action), `hE6one`, `hE6mul`, `hE6int`, `hE6c` (reduced-norm identity as in `hE5c`), `hE6d` ($\hat e'$ restricts to $\hat e$ on $R_2$), `hE6star` ($R_2'$ is star-stable), `hE6inj` ($\hat e'$ is injective) and `hE6f` (for every prime $q \neq r$ and every $z \in R_2'$: if $\hat e'(z)$ annihilates every $q$-torsion point of $A_0$ over every base, then $r^K z \in q\Lambda_1^s$ for some $K$) are imposed.
--
--   **The Hecke frame and the element $s$.** $n_1$ is an adelic unit in $\mathrm{primeHeckeSet}\,R_1\,r$, and `hS₁` requires $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1 = R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ to be an Eichler order of level $Nr$. Further, $\ell$ is a prime different from $r$ and $\bar r$, $s \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ and $s_f$ an adelic unit, subject to the four clauses `hs`: the local component of $s_f$ at every height-one prime not containing $r$ is $s \otimes 1$; its local component at every prime containing $r$ is $1$; the product of the diagonal image of the scalar $\ell$ with $s_f^{-1}$ lies in $\mathrm{levelHeckeUSet}\,\Lambda_1\,(\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1)\,\ell$ if $\ell \mid N$ and in $\mathrm{primeHeckeSet}\,(\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1)\,\ell$ otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s) = \ell$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist: an extra level structure $C_0$ of level $\ell$ on $A_0$ (a closed subscheme of $A_0.A$, stable under the group law, the inverse and the $\Lambda$-action, containing the unit, killed by $\ell$, disjoint from the level-$N$ datum, finite flat of finite presentation of fibre rank $\ell^2$, and geometrically isomorphic to $\mathbb Z/\ell \times \mathbb Z/\ell$); a fake elliptic curve $A_{0}^{s}$ for $(\Lambda,N)$ over $k_0$; morphisms $a_s : A_0.A \to A_{0}^{s}.A$ and $a_s' : A_{0}^{s}.A \to A_0.A$ over the base, a natural number $k_s$, morphisms $b_s : A_{0}^{s}.A \to A_0.A$ and $b_s' : A_0.A \to A_{0}^{s}.A$ with $b_s$ over the base and $a_s$ followed by $b_s$ over the base, and a natural number $m_s$ with $r^{m_s}\,\bar s \in R_2$, such that the following four assertions hold.
--
--   First, $a_s$ and $a_s'$ exhibit $A_{0}^{s}$ as a level-$\ell$ isogenous curve of $(A_0, C_0)$: both are homomorphisms for the relative group laws, both commute with the $\Lambda$-actions, $a_s$ followed by $a_s'$ is multiplication by $\ell$ on $A_0$ and $a_s'$ followed by $a_s$ is multiplication by $\ell$ on $A_{0}^{s}$ (whenever $\ell$ is viewed in $\Lambda$), a point of $A_0$ is killed by $a_s$ precisely when it factors through $C_0$, and $a_s$ carries level-$N$ points to level-$N$ points.
--
--   Second, $(b_s, b_s')$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_s}$ from $A_{0}^{s}$ to $A_0$, and third, $b_s$ preserves the level-$N$ structure.
--
--   Fourth, $a_s$ followed by $b_s$ equals $\hat e\bigl(r^{m_s}\,\bar s\bigr)$, the endomorphism of $A_0$ attached by the order-level dictionary to the element $r^{m_s}\,\mathrm{star}(s)$ of $R_2$.
--
--   Fifth, for every injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of $\{X_0.\mathrm{actEnd}\,a\} \cup \{X_0.\varpi\mathrm{End}\}$ in $\operatorname{End}(X_0.F)$ into $M_2(K_0)$, there is one $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that both of the following hold: (i) for every $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ there are $\varepsilon$ in that centraliser and $k_\gamma \in \mathbb Z$ whose power series, truncated and evaluated at nilpotent parameters, induces through $\theta_0$ the same map as $e\gamma$, and $E_0\varepsilon = r^{k_\gamma}\,g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$; and (ii) there are $\varepsilon_s$ in that centraliser and $k_s' \in \mathbb Z$ whose power series induces through $\theta_0$ the same map as $a_s$ followed by $b_s$, and $E_0\varepsilon_s = r^{k_s'}\,g\,\iota_0(\bar s)\,g^{-1}$, where $\bar s = \mathrm{star}(s)$.
--
--   This is the step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld description of the special fibre in which the dictionary between endomorphisms of the base-point fake elliptic curve and the quaternionic data is extended from the units $\tilde\Gamma$ and the order $R_2$ to a Hecke element $s$ of reduced norm $\ell$: the Hecke correspondence at $\ell$ is realised by a level-$\ell$ isogeny $a_s$ followed by an $r$-power isogeny $b_s$, whose composite matches $\iota_0(\bar s)$ under a single conjugating matrix $g$ serving the whole dictionary. It is used in the construction of the tower family attached to the fine moduli description, through [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_cerednikDrinfeld_towerFamily_liftT_of_rigidifiedToG_of_isNoetherianRing_heightNormalised_eq_oneLegC5`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_cerednikDrinfeld_towerFamily_liftT_of_rigidifiedToG_of_isNoetherianRing_heightNormalised_eq_oneLegC5).
